	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Let <m>f(x)</m> be a continuous function with derivative <m>\displaystyle f'(x)=(x-2)^2(x+3)(x-5)</m>.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task workspace="1.5in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Give all critical numbers for <m>f(x)</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>x=2, \quad x=-3, \quad x=5 .</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task workspace="4in">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Using a sign chart, find the intervals of increasing and decreasing for <m>f(x)</m>. Write your answers using interval notation.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c|c} 
						\amp (-\infty,-3) \amp (-3,2) \amp (2,5) \amp (5,\infty) \\
						(x-2)^2 \amp + \amp + \amp + \amp + \\
						(x+3) \amp - \amp + \amp + \amp + \\
						(x-5) \amp - \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
						f' \amp + \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
						f \amp \nearrow \amp \searrow \amp \searrow \amp \nearrow
						\end{array}
					</me>
					<me>
						f \text{ is increasing on } (-\infty,-3) \cup(5, \infty)
					</me>
					<me>
						f \text{ is decreasing on } (-3,2) \cup(2,5).
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Classify each critical number as a relative minimum, relative maximum, or neither.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					There's a relative max at <m>x=-3</m>, a relative min at <m>x=5</m>, and neither at <m>x=2</m>.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				On the axes below, draw the graph of a continuous function <m>f(x)</m> that satisfies the following:
			</p>
			<p>
				 <m>f(0)=5</m>   <m>f'(0)=0</m>   <m>f'(x)&gt;0</m> on <m>(-\infty, 0)</m>   <m>f'(x)&lt;0</m> on <m>(0,\infty)</m>    <m>f''(x)&lt;0</m> on <m>(-\infty, 2)</m>   <m>f''(x)&gt;0</m> on <m>(2,\infty)</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				<m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}f(x)=1</m>
			</p>
			<p>
				
			</p>
			<p>
				 
			</p>
		</introduction>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				 Farmer Stan is building a rectangular cattle pen to be enclosed by a fence and divided into three equal parts by two
			</p>
			<p>
				 additional fences parallel to one of the sides (see picture below). If he has 200 meters of fencing available to use, what dimensions for the outer rectangle (i.e., what values for <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>) will create the largest possible total area? Justify that your solution is optimal. Show all work.
			</p>
			<p>
				
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				We need to maximize the area
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\amp A=x y=x\left(50-\frac{1}{2} x\right) . \\
					\amp A(x)=50 x-\frac{1}{2} x^2 \\
					\amp A'(x)=50-x=0 \Rightarrow x=50 .
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				We need to determine whether there's a max at <m>x=50</m>.
				<me>
					A''(x)=-1 \Rightarrow A''(50)=-1\lt 0, \text { so it is a max. }
				</me>
				When <m>x=50, y=50-\frac{1}{2} \cdot 50=25</m>.
				The dimensions that will maximize the area are <m>x=50 \mathrm{~m}</m> and <m>y=25 \mathrm{~m}</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Find the intervals of concavity and inflection points (<m>x</m> values only) of <m>f(x)=\frac{1}{2}{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}+12{x}^{2}+17x+89</m>. Give the intervals of concavity using interval notation. Show all work.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					\amp f(x)=\frac{1}{2} x^4-5 x^3+12 x^2+17 x+89 \\
					\amp f'(x)=2 x^3-15 x^2+24 x+17 \\
					\amp f''(x)=6 x^2-30 x+24=6\left(x^2-5 x+4\right) \\
					\amp =6(x-4)(x-1)
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				<me>
					\begin{array}{c|c|c|c|c} 
					\amp (-\infty,1) \amp (1,4) \amp (4,\infty) \\
					6 \amp + \amp + \amp + \\
					(x-4) \amp - \amp - \amp + \\
					(x-1) \amp - \amp + \amp + \\
					f'' \amp + \amp - \amp +
					\end{array}
				</me>
				<m>f</m> is concave up on <m>(-\infty, 1) \cup(4, \infty)</m>
				<m>f</m> is concave down on <m>(1,4)</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				An Etsy necklace maker finds that when they price their necklaces at <m>\$20</m>, they sell <m>100</m> necklaces in a month. They determine that for each <m>\$1</m> price increase, they will sell 4 fewer necklaces per month. If each necklace costs <m>\$5</m> to produce, what price should they charge to maximize profit? Justify your solution is optimal.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<solution>
			<p>
				Let <m>x</m> be the number of <m>\$1</m> price increases.
				Then <m>R(x)=\underbrace{(20+x)}_{\text {price }} \underbrace{(100-4 x)}_{\text {units }}</m> and <m>C(x)=5(100-4 x)</m>.
				Then,
				<me>
					\begin{aligned}
					P(x) \amp =R(x)-C(x) \\
					\amp =(20+x)(100-4 x)-5(100-4 x) \\
					\amp =(100-4 x)(20+x-5) \\
					\amp =(100-4 x)(15+x) \\
					P'(x) \amp =-4(15+x)+(100-4 x) \cdot 1 \\
					\amp =-60-4 x+100-4 x=40-8 x=0 \text { at } x=5.
					\end{aligned}
				</me>
				Notice that there's a max at <m>x=5</m>, since <m>P''(5)=-8 \lt 0</m>.
				Therefore, the price they should charge to maximize the profit is <m>\$(20+5)</m>, that is, <m>\$ 25</m>.
			</p>
		</solution>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					After <m>t</m> days of advertisements for a new restaurant, the proportions of shoppers in a town who have seen the ads is <m>1-e^{-0.04t}.</m> How long must the ads run to reach 90% of the shoppers? You do not need to simplify your answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					We need to know for what value of <m>t</m>
					<me>
						1-e^{-0.04 t}=\frac{90}{100} .
					</me>
					Solving for <m>t</m>, we have
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						1 - \frac{90}{100} \amp =e^{-0.04 t} \\
						\ln \left(1-\frac{90}{100}\right) \amp =-0.04 t \Rightarrow t=\frac{-1}{0.04} \ln \left(1-\frac{90}{100}\right) \text { days. }
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>\dfrac{d}{dt}\ln(f(t))</m> (i.e. the relative rate of change) of <m>f(t)=t^2+1</m> at <m>t=2</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\frac{d}{d t}\left(\ln \left(t^2+1\right)\right)=\frac{1}{t^2+1}\left(t^2+1\right)'=\frac{1}{t^2+1} \cdot 2 t
					</me>
					At <m>t=2</m>,
					<me>
						\frac{d}{d t}(\ln (f(t))=\frac{4}{4+1}=\frac{4}{5} .
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A car worth $15,000 depreciates in value by 15% each year. How much is it worth after 5 years? You do not need to simplify your answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The car is worth
					<m>\$ 15000(1-0.15)^5</m> after 5 years.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
	<exercise>
		<introduction>
			<p>
				Compute the following derivatives.
			</p>
		</introduction>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>f'(x)</m> if <m>f(x) = \ln(x) \sqrt{x}.</m> You do not need to simplify your final answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						f'(x)=\frac{1}{x} \sqrt{x}+\ln (x) \cdot \frac{1}{2} x^{-1 / 2}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
		<task>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Find <m>g''(x)</m> if <m>g(x)=e^{x^3}</m>. You do not need to simplify your final answer.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<me>
						\begin{aligned}
						\amp g'(x)=e^{x^3} \cdot 3 x^2 \\
						\amp g''(x)=e^{x^3} \cdot 3 x^2 \cdot 3 x^2+e^{x^3} \cdot 6 x .
						\end{aligned}
					</me>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</task>
	</exercise>
</worksheet>
